Presidential elections were held in [[East Timor]] on 17 March 2012, with a second round on 16 April.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lefigaro.fr/flash-actu/2012/03/19/97001-20120319FILWWW00326-l-onu-salue-l-election-au-timor-leste.php |title=L'ONU salue l'élection au Timor-Leste |publisher=Le Figaro|date=19 March 2012 |language=French |accessdate=19 April 2012}}</ref> Incumbent president [[José Ramos-Horta]], who was eligible for a second and final term as president, announced that he would seek nomination to be a candidate in the election.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.thejakartaglobe.com/international/east-timors-ramos-horta-to-seek-second-term/494826 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120914003952/http://www.thejakartaglobe.com/international/east-timors-ramos-horta-to-seek-second-term/494826 |url-status=dead |archive-date=14 September 2012 |title=East Timor's Ramos-Horta to Seek Second Term |date=31 January 2012 |work=Jakarta Globe |agency=Agence France-Presse |accessdate=19 April 2012 }}</ref> The election was seen as a test for the "young democracy" in seeking to take control of its own [[security]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.abc.net.au/news/2012-03-17/polls-close-in-east-timor-presidential-election/3896052?site=sydney |title=Polls close in East Timor presidential election |publisher=Australian Broadcasting Corporation |date=17 March 2012|accessdate=16 April 2012}}</ref> Former military commander [[Taur Matan Ruak]] provisionally beat [[Francisco Guterres]] in a second round runoff.
{{Politics of East Timor}}

== Background ==
The [[president of East Timor|presidency]] is seen as a unifying post for the country after the [[1999 East Timorese crisis]]. This election is also seen as a test [[FRETILIN]]'s ability to take back control in the [[2012 East Timorese parliamentary election|parliamentary election]] later in the year.<ref name="aljaz"/>

Incumbent president [[José Ramos-Horta]] had initially been reluctant to run for re-election, but a draft movement collected over 120,000 signatures in favour of his candidacy, prompting him to run.<ref>{{cite web|author=Kommentar posten |url=http://derstandard.at/1326504160304/Praesident-Ramos-Horta-tritt-im-Maerz-erneut-zur-Wahl-an |title=Präsident Ramos-Horta tritt im März erneut zur Wahl an - Osttimor |publisher=Derstandard.at |date=31 January 2012 |language=German |accessdate=16 April 2012}}</ref>

== Candidates ==
There were finally twelve candidates running for president; two others eventually were excluded, one failing to meet the nomination requirements and another dying at the start of the election.

