The candidacy of [[Angela Freitas]],<ref name="antidote">{{cite web |url=https://www.theaustralian.com.au/nation/world/doctor-offers-antidote-to-east-timors-ills/news-story/cbcb65eca7e48afb6455c1bf55994339 |title=Doctor offers antidote to East Timor's ills |author=Ted McDonell |date=16 January 2012 |work=The Australian |archive-date=2012-01-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120115191211/http://www.theaustralian.com.au/news/world/doctor-offers-antidote-to-east-timors-ills/story-e6frg6so-1226244844619 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |access-date=19 April 2012}}</ref> was rejected by the CNE due to the ineligibility of some of the signatures required.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.cjitl.org/cjitltimor-today/cjitl-gender/924 |title=Angela: Rejected Presidential candidate predicts more violence in East Timor |publisher=Cjitl.org |date=20 February 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120225022248/http://www.cjitl.org/cjitltimor-today/cjitl-gender/924 |archive-date=25 February 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://cjitl.org/cjitltimor-today/cjitl-breaking-news/944 |title=Angela: I may have lost this battle but the "War" is far from over |publisher=Cjitl.org |date=23 February 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120224192937/http://www.cjitl.org/cjitltimor-today/cjitl-breaking-news/944 |archive-date=24 February 2012 |url-status=dead}}</ref> She then offered her support to FRETILIN's<ref>{{cite web |last=McDonnell |first=Ted |url=http://www.demotix.com/news/1096151/unity-east-timor-presidential-race |title=Unity in East Timor Presidential race |publisher=Demotix.com |accessdate=16 April 2012 }}{{Dead link|date=April 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> Francisco Guterres. One candidate [[Francisco Xavier do Amaral]],<ref name="smf"/> a member of the [[National Parliament of East Timor]] and a leader of [[Timorese Social Democratic Association]], died during the campaign, on 5 March 2012.<ref name=australian>{{cite news|first=Peter|last=Alford|title=Resistance-era president of East Timor, Francisco Xavier do Amaral, dies in Dili, aged 74 |url=https://www.theaustralian.com.au/news/world/resistance-era-president-of-east-timor-francisco-xavier-do-amaral-dies-in-dili-aged-74/news-story/b4919e82ccf1f3ac568f494006f6a27c |work=[[The Australian]] |date=17 March 2012 |archive-date=2012-03-07|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120307060033/http://www.theaustralian.com.au/news/world/resistance-era-president-of-east-timor-francisco-xavier-do-amaral-dies-in-dili-aged-74/story-e6frg6so-1226291130113|url-access=subscription|url-status=live|access-date=6 April 2012}}</ref> A special plenary session of parliament amended the electoral so as not to restart the electoral process<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.theaustralian.com.au/news/world/resistance-era-president-of-east-timor-francisco-xavier-do-amaral-dies-in-dili-aged-74/news-story/b4919e82ccf1f3ac568f494006f6a27c |title=Resistance-era president of East Timor, Francisco Xavier do Amaral, dies in Dili, aged 74 |author=Peter Alford |date=7 March 2012 |work=The Australian |archive-date=2012-03-07 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120307060033/http://www.theaustralian.com.au/news/world/resistance-era-president-of-east-timor-francisco-xavier-do-amaral-dies-in-dili-aged-74/story-e6frg6so-1226291130113 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |access-date=19 April 2012}}</ref> as mandated by the older law. The controversial move elicited protests.{{Citation needed|date=March 2012}}

==Campaign==
Economic issues were said to be the primary concern.<ref name="issue"/> According to the ''[[Agence France-Presse|AFP]]'', the most likely candidates were José Ramos-Horta, Francisco "Lu Olo" Guterres and Taur Matan Ruak. Ruak also said that he was confident of winning without a run-off. He won the backing of CNRT after it withdrew support for Ramos-Horta over criticism of the government; still, Ramos-Horta said he was not unhappy with the decision to support Ruak: "I'm very happy he's supporting one of my favourite candidates. If someone supports [Ruak] I'm happy because I admire [him]."<ref name="Afp">{{cite web|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5hbbLpk49MI7q4i8WAwos_zZj4LtA?docId=CNG.527f1a997d82d05d300c1ca083806053.141 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130124161808/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5hbbLpk49MI7q4i8WAwos_zZj4LtA?docId=CNG.527f1a997d82d05d300c1ca083806053.141 |url-status=dead |archive-date=24 January 2013 |title=AFP: East Timor votes in test for young nation |date=16 March 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012}}</ref> Ruak was seen as likely to get some votes from FRETILIN's traditional base as he was popular with the party and repeatedly courted by the party hierarchy before opting to run as an independent after he quit his army post. However, there were also concerns of having someone affiliated to the army run for president because the army has sometimes disagreed with the police.<ref name="smf"/> Ruak campaigned wearing camouflage to highlight his role in the [[Indonesian occupation of East Timor|War of Independence]].<ref name="result"/>

Ramos-Horta said before the election that "If I'm not elected, I have so many things to do - I have to struggle to choose what to do."<ref name="sky"/> Xanana Gusmão campaigned for Ruak. Both Guitteres and Ruak said that they would abide by the outcome of the second round of the election.<ref name="winner"/> Ruak also said that he was proud to be Gusmão's deputy during the independence struggle.<ref name="aus"/> In the second round the Democratic Party and Ramos-Horta remained neutral.<ref name="smk">Kingsbury, Damien {{cite web|url=http://www.smh.com.au/opinion/political-news/timor-election-a-key-test-of-stability-20120417-1x52p.html |title=Timor election a key test of stability |date=17 April 2012 |publisher=National Times, SMH.com.au |accessdate=19 April 2012}}</ref>

==Monitors and electoral issues==
International observers were present from Australia, the European Union, Asian Network for Free Elections (ANFREL) and [[Lusophone]] states.<ref name="Afp"/> On 2 March, the CNE held a meeting, led by its president, Faustino Cardoso Gomes, with an EU delegation based in the country to discuss issues pertaining to the election and its monitoring.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.cne.tl/index.php/main/index/en/information/news_meeting_eu.php |title=Meeting with the European Union about electoral situation |publisher=National Electoral Commission |date=2 March 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161003201606/http://www.cne.tl/index.php/main/index/en/information/news_meeting_eu.php |archive-date=3 October 2016 |url-status=dead}}</ref> The Timor-Leste Friendship Network/[[Deakin University]] Observer Mission was an Australian observer group.<ref name="smk"/>

On 28 February, the [[National Electoral Commission (East Timor)|National Electoral Commission]] (CNE) had organised a national initiative for the conduct of a peaceful election at the [[Dili Convention Centre]]. It was attended by traditional leaders from the 13 electoral districts and culminated in a pact being signed after a debate by the then 12 of 13 candidates (Francisco Xavier do Amaral did not attend the event due to ill health).<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.cne.tl/index.php/main/index/en/information/news_national_pact.php |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120722090919/http://www.cne.tl/index.php/main/index/en/information/news_national_pact.php |url-status=dead |archive-date=22 July 2012 |title=National Pact for Peaceful Elections 2012 |publisher=National Electoral Commission |date=28 February 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012 }}</ref>

On 5 March, the CNE held a meeting with the political parties and presidential candidates to explain issues and rules about the campaigning process, the roles of political parties' and candidates' agents and [[campaign finance]] issues. The president of state-owned<ref name="afp2"/> [[Radio-Televisão Timor Leste]] (RTTL) Expedito also elucidated RTTL's role in covering the election. Amongst the attendees were representatives of all 13 political parties and six presidential candidates.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.cne.tl/index.php/main/index/en/information/news_meetings_pp4th.php |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120801143951/http://www.cne.tl/index.php/main/index/en/information/news_meetings_pp4th.php |url-status=dead |archive-date=1 August 2012 |title=CNE's 4th meeting with the political parties and candidates |publisher=National Electoral Commission |date=5 March 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012 }}</ref>

The Deputy Secretary-General of [[UNMIT]], [[Finn Reske-Nielson]], said of the election that "we have seen a clean campaign that's been virtually free of violence. Every candidate has committed him or herself to peace."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.irinnews.org/Report/95079/TIMOR-LESTE-High-hopes-for-post-election-prosperity |title=TIMOR-LESTE: High hopes for post-election prosperity |publisher=IRIN |date=16 March 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012}}</ref> If the election turned out as planned UNMIT could also leave the country at the end of the year as per its current mandate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.globalpost.com/dispatch/news/regions/asia-pacific/120316/east-timor-elections-martial-arts-groups |title=East Timor elections: what's at stake |author=Kate Lamb |work=GlobalPost |date=16 March 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012}}</ref>

==Violence==
On 20 February, the offices of the CNE and the Technical Secretariat for Electoral Administration were firebombed at about 3:00 in the national capital of Dili. The attack also damaged a UNMIT vehicle.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://news.smh.com.au/breaking-news-world/arson-attack-on-east-timor-election-office-20120223-1tpn6.html |title=Arson attack on East Timor election office |work=The Sydney Morning Herald|author=Karlis Salna |date=23 February 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012}}</ref> Australia's ''[[Sky News]]'' also suggested that witnesses had reported seeing serving members of the military campaign for Ruak amid what it said were fears of voter intimidation. However, Ramos-Horta said that he is "completely reassured about security. Our police and the United Nations police are alert all over the country. They have tremendous experience over the years in assessing the situation, in pre-empting any security threats so I am very confident it will be okay."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.skynews.com.au/topstories/article.aspx?id=726372&vId= |title=Reports of voter intimidation in E Timor |publisher=Sky News |date=7 March 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012}}</ref> However, Indonesia's ambassador, Eddy Setyabudi, said that the [[government of Indonesia]] was making contingency plans to evacuate 7,540 Indonesians from the country if the situation worsens and riots occur, following a meeting of the Indonesian-East Timor Border Coordination committee.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.tempo.co.id/hg/nasional/2012/03/09/brk,20120309-389130,uk.html |title=Govt Prepares Evacuation of RI Citizens from East Timor |author=Yohanes Seo and Jose Sarito Amaral |work=Tempo |date=9 March 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305092242/http://tempo.co.id/hg/nasional/2012/03/09/brk,20120309-389130,uk.html |archive-date=5 March 2016 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

On 13 April, just before the second round, about 100 people were reported to have pelted Ruak's campaign headquarters with stones. More violence was reported over the burning of the homes of two "political supporters" and several campaign vehicles. National Police (PNTL) deputy commander Alfonso de Jesus said that due to the presence of police untoward incidents were not serious: "We had six police officers there but [if] straight away they are not respecting the police presence there then the group is using the opportunity to take stones and throw them at the office."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theaustralian.com.au/news/latest-news/mob-attacks-east-timor-candidates-office/news-story/3d7b849d9e34117614ef7b3d0ceb1796 |title=Mob attacks East Timor candidate's office |agency=Australian Associated Press |work=The Australian |date=14 April 2012 |archive-date=2012-04-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120415035035/http://www.theaustralian.com.au/news/world/mob-attacks-east-timor-candidates-office/story-fn3dxix6-1226326571762 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |access-date=19 April 2012}}</ref>

==Results==

About 620,000 people<ref name="2ndround"/> out of a population of 1,100,000 are able to vote.<ref name="aljaz"/> There are 13 electoral districts in the country.<ref name="sky">{{cite web|url=http://www.skynews.com.au/world/article.aspx?id=730122&vId=3131013 |title=Ramos-Horta voted out in East Timor |publisher=Sky News |date=18 March 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012}}</ref> Voting took place between 7:00 and 15:00 with no reports of untoward incidents. In at least one polling station that was monitored, the attendants unsealed the ballots delivered under United Nations Police protection. A high voter turnout was also recorded with some polling stations needing to use their reserve ballots.<ref name="Afp"/>

[[Ermenegildo Lopes]], the leader of the [[Bloku Ploklamador]], said that his party's representatives indicated that no one would cross the 50% threshold to avoid a run-off. The ''AFP'' also said the election was "remarkably organised" in contrast to the [[2007 East Timorese presidential election|pre-election violence in 2006]].<ref name="afp2">{{cite web|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5jMplRMSDDLk_D_HwY5i30O9CTaVA?docId=CNG.7e0b7b93a2e67c1152731dc62d9b5a24.4a1 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130125163356/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5jMplRMSDDLk_D_HwY5i30O9CTaVA?docId=CNG.7e0b7b93a2e67c1152731dc62d9b5a24.4a1 |url-status=dead |archive-date=25 January 2013 |title=AFP: Ramos-Horta out as East Timor heads for run-off |date=17 March 2012 |accessdate=16 April 2012}}</ref> Ramos-Horta said of the election that "there has not been a single incidence of violence" and that he continues to believe the country is already ready to take control of its security.<ref name="sky"/>

The preliminary hand-counted result, broadcast live on RTTL,<ref name="afp2"/> of over 70% of the votes indicated that Francisco Guterres was leading with 27.28% of the votes, followed by Taur Matan Ruak with 24.17% of the votes and incumbent president José Ramos-Horta followed with 19.43% of the votes<ref name="issue"/> (though Amaral had died, his name was still on the result notification issued by the election commission<!--see template source below-->). Provisional results for the second round were scheduled to be announced within 48 hours of the polls closing,<ref name="2ndround">{{cite web|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/asia-pacific/2012/04/201241612630412630.html |title=East Timor votes for new president - Asia-Pacific |publisher=Al Jazeera English |date=4 October 2011 |accessdate=19 April 2012}}</ref> though they were announced a day earlier.<ref name="winner"/> Though the final result had to be certified by the Court of Appeals, Ruak had got 61.23% of the votes.<ref name="result">{{cite web|url=http://www.aljazeera.com/news/asia-pacific/2012/04/2012417234516737874.html |title=Matan Ruak 'wins' East Timor polls - Asia-Pacific |publisher=Al Jazeera English |date= 10 April 2011 |accessdate=19 April 2012}}</ref> Turnout was 72.1%.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.rulers.org/2012-04.html |title=April 2012 |publisher=Rulers.org |accessdate=19 April 2012}}</ref>

